# Admin data

Name coherentsearch

Usage "Search normalised FFTs for pulsars by coherently summing many harmonics and scoring the folded profiles with boxcar matched filters."

Version [exec date +%d%b%y]

Commandline full_cmd_line

# Options (in order you want them to appear)

Int -ncpus   ncpus      {Number of processors to use with OpenMP} \
	-r 1 oo  -d 1
Flag -deep deep         {Deep pass for slow, very narrow pulses: -nharms 120 -maxdecim 12 -hifreq 5 unless those are given, writing <input>_deep.cohout}
Double -threshold threshold {S/N cutoff for candidates} \
	-r 0.0 oo  -d 8.0
Int -nharms  nharms     {Number of harmonics to sum coherently (60 is divisible by 2-6, so it decimates evenly)} \
	-r 2 1024  -d 60
Int -maxdecim maxdecim  {Largest harmonic-decimation factor k: also fold nharms/k harmonics at k times each fundamental (1 disables it)} \
	-r 1 64  -d 6
Double -lofreq lofreq   {Lowest fundamental frequency (Hz) to search} \
	-r 0.0 oo  -d 0.1
Int -lobin   lobin      {Lowest Fourier bin to search (overrides -lofreq)} \
	-r 0 oo
Double -hifreq hifreq   {Highest fundamental frequency (Hz) to search.  Decimation extends coverage to hifreq * maxdecim} \
	-r 0.0 oo  -d 125.0
Double -hidr hidr       {Trial spacing (Fourier bins) at the highest harmonic} \
	-r 0.0 oo  -d 0.5
Int -m       m          {Fourier bins in the interpolation kernel (even)} \
	-r 2 1024  -d 16
Flag -measuredsigma measuredsigma {Measure each chunk's noise scale (robust MAD) instead of computing it from a normalised FFT}
Int -ncands  ncands     {Maximum number of candidates to report} \
	-r 1 oo  -d 100
Double -drtol drtol     {Fourier-bin tolerance for collapsing near-identical candidates} \
	-r 0.0 oo  -d 1.0
Int -numharm numharm    {Highest harmonic number when collapsing harmonically related candidates} \
	-r 1 1024  -d 16
Flag -noremove noremove {Do not collapse near-identical candidates}
Flag -noharmremove noharmremove {Do not collapse harmonically related candidates}
Flag -noperiodic noperiodic {Keep candidates whose profile is dominated by one high harmonic (harmonic 7 or above, with over 8x the power of each of harmonics 1-4)}
Int -blocksize blocksize {Trial fundamentals per chunk} \
	-r 1 oo  -d 2048
String -o outfile       {Output candidate file name (single input file only; default: <input>.cohout beside it)}
String -outdir outdir   {Write each <input>.cohout into this directory instead}
Flag -plot plot         {Plot the candidates' pulse profiles to <input>_cohout.ps (and, with Ghostscript, to <input>_cohout_01.png, _02.png, ...)}
Flag -replot replot     {Do not search: plot the candidates already in <input>.cohout}
Flag -rewrite rewrite   {Do not search: re-check every candidate in <input>.cohout against the pulsar database and rewrite the file with the matches labelled (add -replot to plot them too)}
Flag -noprogress noprogress {Do not print a progress meter}
Flag -verbose verbose   {Report the trial grid and chunking before searching}

# Rest of command line:

Rest infile {Input '.fft' file(s), each with a matching '.inf' file.  They should be normalised (e.g. by rednoise) so that Fourier powers have mean 1} \
        -c 1 16384

Description {coherentsearch searches normalised Fourier power spectra
.RI ( .fft
files, normally from
.BR realfft (1)
followed by
.BR rednoise (1))
for periodic signals.  Unlike
.BR accelsearch (1),
which adds harmonic
.I powers
incoherently, it interpolates the complex amplitude of every harmonic
exactly, inverse-FFTs the harmonic stack into a pulse profile, and
scores that profile with a bank of boxcar matched filters.  Keeping the
phases makes it most sensitive to narrow pulses, which put their power
into many harmonics.  It is a C port of the production search in
CoherentSearch.jl (Ransom 2026, arXiv:2610.11749).
.PP
Every trial fundamental, spaced so that the highest harmonic moves by
.B -hidr
bins, is folded with
.B -nharms
harmonics.  Harmonic decimation then re-uses the same amplitudes to fold
every k-th harmonic
.RB ( "k = 2 .. -maxdecim" )
at k times each fundamental, almost for free.  The profile S/N is
normalised by a noise scale computed from the number of harmonics
summed, which assumes Fourier powers of mean 1; a check on a few chunks
warns if the data disagree, and
.B -measuredsigma
estimates the scale from the data instead.
.PP
.B -ncpus
searches independent chunks of trial frequencies in parallel; the
results do not depend on the number of threads.  The small batched FFTs
are fastest with FFTW wisdom from
.BR makewisdom ,
which covers the default
.B -nharms
and
.BR -blocksize ;
without it they are planned with FFTW_ESTIMATE, which is slower but
equally deterministic.
.PP
Candidates above
.B -threshold
are collapsed (near-identical frequencies, then harmonically related
ones).  Those whose refolded profile is dominated by a single high
harmonic (the strongest is harmonic 7 or above, with over 8 times the
power of each of harmonics 1 to 4) are then dropped, unless
.B -noperiodic
is given.  Such a profile is a comb of identical peaks: almost always a
strong signal, such as a bright pulsar or its harmonics, landing on a high
harmonic of a slow trial.  The strongest
.B -ncands
are written to
.IR <input>.cohout ,
with each one's S/N, frequency, period, number of harmonics summed and
best-matching duty cycle.  Candidates whose frequency matches a harmonic
of a known pulsar near the observation's position (from PRESTO's pulsar
database, as in
.BR accelsearch (1))
are labelled in an extra column, and in the plots.
.B -plot
also draws every reported candidate's pulse profile to
.IR <input>_cohout.ps ,
refolded at the
full
.B -nharms
depth with a wide interpolation kernel, 15 to a page.  If Ghostscript
.RB ( gs )
is available, those pages are also rendered as PNGs
.RI ( <input>_cohout_01.png ,
.IR _02.png ,
\&...), as
.BR prepfold (1)
does.
.B -replot
redraws them from an existing
.I .cohout
file without searching again, labelling known pulsars afresh.
.B -rewrite
re-checks every candidate in an existing
.I .cohout
file against the pulsar database and rewrites the file with the matches
labelled, again without searching.  It reads only the
.I .inf
file, not the
.IR .fft ,
so it is quick; add
.B -replot
to plot them as well.  Both read the files that CoherentSearch.jl writes,
so a large search run there (on a GPU, say) can be labelled and plotted
here with
.B coherentsearch -rewrite -replot
.IR *_red.fft .
.PP
.B -deep
runs the deep pass recommended in the paper for slow pulsars with very
narrow pulses (duty cycles below about 1%), which the default 120-bin
profiles do not resolve: fundamentals up to 5 Hz folded with 120
harmonics (240-bin profiles) and decimation factors up to 12.  It costs
roughly a third as much as the default search.  Any of
.BR -nharms ,
.B -maxdecim
or
.B -hifreq
given explicitly overrides the deep value.  Its candidates go to
.I <input>_deep.cohout
(and its plots to
.IR <input>_deep_cohout.ps ),
so that it can be run beside a default search of the same file; the two
candidate lists together are the paper's \(lqdefault plus deep pass\(rq
search.  If that default search's
.I <input>.cohout
exists (in the same directory), deep candidates harmonically related to a
stronger candidate in it are dropped, such as the subharmonics of a
bright pulsar whose own frequency lies above the deep band (unless
.B -noharmremove
is given).  So we
recommend running
.B -deep
after a regular search of the same file.
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
realfft mypulsar_DM10.00.dat
rednoise mypulsar_DM10.00.fft
coherentsearch mypulsar_DM10.00_red.fft
coherentsearch -deep mypulsar_DM10.00_red.fft
.fi
.RE
.PP
To sift the candidates from a search over many DMs (and, optionally, many
observations), run
.B coherent_sift.py
on the
.I .cohout
files (or on the directory holding them).  It groups each signal's
detections across DM and observation, flags harmonics, known pulsars,
edge-peaked and broadband (RFI-like) signals, and ranks the rest by how
pulsar-like they look; see
.BR "coherent_sift.py --help" .
}

Author {Scott Ransom <sransom@nrao.edu>}

SeeAlso {accelsearch(1), realfft(1), rednoise(1), prepfold(1)}
